        "type": "article",
        "title": "Notes from June 2026",
        "summary": "Chicago’s weather is pretty lousy most of the year, but when it’s nice, it’s *very* nice. June blessed the city with dozens of idyllic days. But don’t worry—I still spent most of the time inside on th",
        "content": "Chicago’s weather is pretty lousy most of the year, but when it’s nice, it’s *very* nice. June blessed the city with dozens of idyllic days. But don’t worry—I still spent most of the time inside on the computer.\n\n## Things I did\n\nI launched my first big project at Ghost: [automations](https://ghost.org/changelog/email-sequences-for-new-members/)! It’s still in beta, but it’s one of the biggest projects I’ve led. If you happen to be a Ghost publisher, please try it out and let me know what you think!\n\nFor the sixteenth issue of the *Taper* online magazine, [I visualized time by breaking each unit into sixteenths](https://evanhahn.com/sixteenth-of-a-year/). For example, as I write this, I’m about 6 sixteenths through the hour. Like every *Taper* submission, my work had to be under 2048 bytes.\n\n## Links about AI ethics\n\nGenerative AI continues to, mostly, be a force for bad in this world:\n\n- “If AI is going to 10x our productivity across the board, that means that I should be able to produce the same amount of output by midday on Monday that, in the before times, would have taken all week. So can I just take Friday off?” From [“Can we have the day off?”](https://mlsu.io/posts/day-off/).\n\n- [“AI’s PR Problem”](https://blog.dshr.org/2026/05/ais-pr-problem.html) has a partial, but scathing, enumeration of many of the problems caused by generative AI. Useful as a reference.\n\n- New AI resistance strategy: [get a religious exemption](https://www.businessinsider.com/worker-got-religious-exemption-using-ai-at-work-2026-6).\n\n- I liked this quote from 1976’s *Computer Power and Human Reason*: “The myth of technological and political and social inevitability is a powerful tranquilizer of the conscience.”\n\n- The Ladybird browser is [no longer accepting patches from the public, due to AI](https://ladybird.org/posts/changing-how-we-develop-ladybird/).\n\n## Other computer links I found\n\n- I think targeted advertising should be illegal, so I loved seeing [“Why Don’t We Just Ban Targeted Advertising?”](https://www.wired.com/story/why-dont-we-just-ban-targeted-advertising/) in a major publication (*WIRED*).\n\n- [Homebrew](https://brew.sh/) creator Mike McQuaid created [a website promoting the practice of doing open source work on company time](https://ossresistance.com/), a practice I agree with. “Open Source software is not a ‘hobby’ for your spare time. Literally every company you have worked for couldn’t run their business without any OSS. They extract value every hour and then ask maintainers to beg for a Friday afternoon, a donation button or a kind word in an all-hands.”\n\n- [Solidarity with Wiki Workers United.](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Wiki_Workers_United_solidarity)\n\n- [`DO_NOT_TRACK`](https://donottrack.sh/) is a proposed environment variable that disables tracking.\n\n- [“Build a web application that works on a playstation portable on a 3G connection—if you do, it will work for all your users, and it will still work 30 years from now.”](https://mohkohn.co.uk/writing/html-first/)\n\n- “At some point you have to actually weave the gossamer. You have to contribute to the infrastructure itself, not just advocate for it.” [Hell yeah.](https://brennan.day/announcing-folk-zone-an-attempt-to-build-the-indieweb-commons-myself/)\n\n- After our fascist president shuttered Climate.gov, it was [reborn by former members of the team](https://www.climate.us/news-features/feed/climateus-launches-independent-website-trusted-climate-information).\n\n- Good [thread about hardware-based attestation](https://grapheneos.social/@GrapheneOS/116550899908879585). “The purpose of these systems is disallowing people from using hardware and software not approved by Apple or Google. This is wrongly presented as being a security feature.” (I actually think there *are* [legitimate uses for hardware attestation](https://signal.org/blog/building-faster-oram/), but not like this.)\n\n## Wacky weird links\n\n- Mikhail Gorbachev, former Soviet Union leader, was in a [Pizza Hut commercial](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mikhail_Gorbachev_Pizza_Hut_commercial)!?\n\n- [Symlinking `CLAUDE.md` to `/dev/urandom`](https://github.com/dwebagents/AgentPipe/pull/49) is good chaos.\n\n- [How to make an HTTP request from the command line, without `curl`.](https://mareksuppa.com/til/bash-dev-tcp-http-without-curl/)\n\n- The maximum size of a PDF page is about 150 square kilometers. [Here’s what that looks like if it were placed over Germany.](https://old.reddit.com/r/MapPorn/comments/18frh7h/)\n\nHope you had a good June.",
        "url": "https://evanhahn.com/notes-from-june-2026/",
        "source": "Evan Hahn (dot com)",
        "tags": [
          "Web Dev",
          "Systems",
          "DevOps"
        ],
        "published_at": "Tue, 30 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000",
        "reading_time": 4,
        "translated_title": null,
        "translated_summary": null,
        "translated_content": null
